---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const buildStack = `use dotcfg::{ConfigStack, DotCfg};

let defaults = DotCfg::new("myapp").at_dir("./config/defaults");
let project = DotCfg::new("myapp").at_dir("./config/project");

let cfg = ConfigStack::new()
    .layer("defaults", defaults)
    .layer("project", project)
    .build()?;`;

const defaultsFile = `[server]
host = "127.0.0.1"
port = 3000`;

const projectFile = `[server]
port = 8080`;

const result = `server.host = "127.0.0.1"
server.port = 8080`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Getting started with ConfigStack — dotcfg"
  description="Learn when you need more than one configuration source and build your first ConfigStack."
>
  <article class="[&_p]:max-w-[720px]">
    <span class="text-[13px] font-bold text-primary">ConfigStack</span>
    <h1 class="m-0 mb-5 mt-3 text-[clamp(38px,7vw,64px)] leading-[1.02] tracking-[-0.045em] max-[760px]:text-[44px]">
      Getting started with ConfigStack
    </h1>
    <p class="max-w-[680px] text-xl text-muted-foreground max-[760px]:text-lg">
      Most applications can use one <code>DotCfg</code> and one config file. Sometimes, though,
      your settings come from more than one file and some of those settings should override others.
      <code>ConfigStack</code> is for that case.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      When one config file is not enough
    </h2>
    <p>
      Imagine an application with a file containing its normal defaults. A project using the
      application wants a different port, but it should not have to copy every default setting just
      to change one value.
    </p>
    <p>The defaults file contains:</p>
    <CodeBlock label="defaults/config.toml" code={defaultsFile} />
    <p>The project file only changes the port:</p>
    <CodeBlock label="project/config.toml" code={projectFile} />
    <p>What the application needs to see is:</p>
    <CodeBlock label="Effective configuration" code={result} />
    <p>
      The host still comes from the defaults file, while the project's port wins. ConfigStack lets
      you describe that relationship instead of manually loading the files and combining them
      yourself.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Build your first stack
    </h2>
    <CodeBlock label="Rust" code={buildStack} />
    <p>
      Each call to <code>layer()</code> adds one <code>DotCfg</code> source and gives it a name.
      Here we called them <code>defaults</code> and <code>project</code>. The names let you refer to
      a particular source later when you need to.
    </p>
    <Callout type="info" title="What is a layer?">
      <p>
        A layer is simply one named <code>DotCfg</code> source inside the stack. The word describes
        how the sources are combined: values from later layers can sit on top of values from earlier
        ones.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      Order matters
    </h2>
    <p>
      Layers are read in the order you add them. If two layers contain the same setting, the later
      layer wins. In the example above, <code>project</code> comes after <code>defaults</code>, so
      its <code>server.port</code> value becomes the one your application reads.
    </p>
    <div class="my-[18px] flex items-center gap-3 rounded-[9px] border border-border bg-card p-4 max-[760px]:flex-col max-[760px]:items-start">
      <span class="font-bold">defaults</span>
      <b class="text-primary">→</b>
      <span class="font-bold">project</span>
      <b class="text-primary">→</b>
      <span class="font-bold">final value</span>
    </div>

    <h2 class="mb-0 mt-12 text-[26px] leading-[1.15] tracking-[-0.025em] max-[760px]:text-2xl">
      DotCfg or ConfigStack?
    </h2>
    <p>
      If your application has one config file, keep using <code>DotCfg</code>. ConfigStack does not
      replace it. Use ConfigStack when you genuinely have multiple file-backed configuration
      sources and need one place to read the final values.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next, learn <a class="font-bold text-primary" href="/config-stack/resolution">how layers are combined</a>,
      including nested settings and environment variables.
    </p>
  </article>
</DocsLayout>
